Not further productive. I'll compile the final report now with everything gathered across both passes, clearly marking measured vs. not-reached.

## Final Report: Device-per-person ground truth

**Method note:** WebSearch was unavailable all session (budget exhausted before first call). Data below comes from direct WebFetch on guessed/known URLs, `curl` against Hacker News Algolia API, GitHub Issues API (`gh api`), OpenAlex, Crossref, and the Wikipedia search/content API. Every figure below is either MEASURED (with source+date) or explicitly marked NOT REACHED — none are recalled from training memory as a substitute.

### 1. Devices per person — the mean vs. the floor

- **Cisco Annual Internet Report** (cisco.com): global networked devices per capita **2.4 (2018) → 3.6 (2023)**. 2023 regional: North America 13.4/capita, W. Europe 9.4, CEE 4.0, Asia-Pacific 3.1, Latin America 3.1, Middle East/Africa 1.5. Global device count 18.4B (2018) → 29.3B (2023), 10% CAGR. **Confidence: high.** This confirms the figure already in hand — matches the user's premise exactly.
- **Deloitte** household device *count* (the ~21-22/household figure): **NOT REACHED.** The 2025 Connected Consumer Survey page that loaded gave spend data only ($896/yr on connected devices in the past year, up from $764 in 2024; $183/mo on tech services) — no device-count figure surfaced.
- **ITU 2024** (itu.int): mobile ownership (age 10+) — global ~4 in 5 own a phone; **high-income countries 95%+, low-income countries only 56%**. That means in low-income countries ~44% of people own *zero* phones of their own — the true floor beneath "single device" is "no device, borrowed access." **Confidence: high.**

### 2. The single-device / no-device population — the key distributional finding

**Pew Research** (pewresearch.org/internet/fact-sheet/mobile/), "smartphone-only" internet users (no home broadband) by US household income:

| Income | 2013 | 2015 | 2018 | 2021 | 2025 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| <$30k | 12% | 20% | 31% | 27% | **34%** |
| $30–70k | 9% | 15% | 22% | 19% | 19% |
| $70–100k | 5% | 10% | 14% | 6% | 10% |
| $100k+ | 5% | 6% | 9% | 6% | 4% |

**Confidence: high.** This is the single most load-bearing number for the identity model under evaluation: over a third of low-income US households in 2025 are down to one device, and that bracket's trend is *worsening* (27%→34% from 2021 to 2025) while the top bracket trends toward more redundancy (falling to 4%). The gap is widening, not converging — a per-device credential model degrades for exactly the population least able to absorb a lost second factor.

**Global mobile subscriptions** (Wikipedia "Mobile phone," sourced): 9.1B subscriptions by 2024 (more than world population), but only ~7.2B people (roughly half the world's countries counted) actually own at least one phone — subscriptions overcount because of the multi-SIM/multi-device population layered on top of the zero-device population.

### 3. Phone sharing (Global South) — partial, one hard number

- **GSMA Mobile Gender Gap** direct sharing-rate percentages for India/Bangladesh/Africa: **NOT REACHED** (gsma.com URLs all 403'd across both passes).
- Via Wikipedia "Gender digital divide": Sub-Saharan Africa mobile-ownership gender gap **13% (2020), down from 14% (2018)**; >74 million SSA women remain unconnected by mobile; SSA internet penetration 2019: men 33.8% vs women 22.6%; the *internet* usage gender gap (as opposed to ownership) grew from 20.7% (2013) to 37% (2019). Arab States/Asia-Pacific internet-penetration gender gap ~17%; digital-skills gap up to 25% in parts of Asia/Middle East. **Confidence: medium** (Wikipedia-mediated, but citing GSMA/ITU as primary sources).
- Via Wikipedia "Digital agriculture": **GSMA 2016** — of 750M+ farmers across 69 surveyed countries, ~295M had a mobile phone; only 13M had *both* a phone and a mobile-money account. **Confidence: high** for this specific figure.
- Via Wikipedia "Digital divide by continent": in Uganda, urban gender gap in mobile ownership is 4% vs rural 22% — a concrete country-level sharing/ownership asymmetry. **Confidence: medium.**
- Explicit phone-**sharing** rates (as opposed to ownership gaps) for India/Bangladesh specifically: **NOT REACHED.**

### 4. Loss, theft, replacement

- **London phone theft** (Wikipedia "Crime in London," citing Met Police data): **91,481 recorded thefts in 2019 → 117,211 in 2024**, a **~28% five-year rise**; 2024 rate works out to **~321 phones/day**. Separately, a BBC News item (Oct 6, 2025) cited on that page: one iPhone tracking case led police to a gang that had sent **40,000 snatched phones to China**. **Confidence: high** for the annual totals (attributed to Met/ONS-style recorded-crime data); the 40,000 figure is from a single case report, not a rate.
- **Moped-enabled crime, London** (Wikipedia "Moped crime in London"): 2011: 365 incidents → 2017 peak: 23,000+ → 2018: 14,300 → 2021: under 4,000 (~50% down from peak). 2016/17: 19,385 moped-enabled crimes recorded; one documented case of a single offender stealing 21 phones in one hour (Oct 2016); a 2017 London Evening Standard estimate put gang-perpetrated moped crime at 50,000+/year with up to 1,500 scooters/motorcycles stolen per month (+41% YoY at the time). **Confidence: medium** — later figures (2018-2021) are solid recorded-crime stats; the 50,000/year and 1,500/month figures are a single 2017 press estimate, not police data.
- **Phone replacement cycle**: Wikipedia "Mobile phone" states "the average user replaces their mobile phone every 11 to 18 months" (uncited claim on the page — flagged as needing verification). This **conflicts** with the general industry narrative of lengthening 3-4 year replacement cycles that the task brief assumed; I could not independently verify either figure from Counterpoint/CIRP/Kantar this session (all attempts 403/404/stub pages). **Confidence: low** — flagging the disagreement rather than picking a side. Counterpoint/CIRP/Kantar primary data: **NOT REACHED.**
- **Google 2019 "basic account hygiene" effectiveness numbers** (SMS/on-device prompt/security-key block rates against bots/phishing/targeted attacks): **NOT REACHED** — only the title and May 17, 2019 publish date came through; the actual percentages did not load via WebFetch, and I found no alternate mirror via curl.
- General account-lockout-after-device-loss data (Google/Microsoft published rates): **NOT REACHED.**

### 5. Shared / kiosk / headless-device patterns

- **RFC 8628 (OAuth Device Authorization Grant)**, confirmed via datatracker.ietf.org: device-code + user-code + verification-URI + polling flow; **Section 5.4 explicitly names phishing as a foresee­able risk** — the spec tells implementers to require the user to confirm device possession during authorization and to keep user-code lifetime short specifically "to limit the usability of a code obtained for phishing." This is a spec-acknowledged weakness, not an emergent one. **Confidence: high** (primary source).
- **Storm-2372 device-code phishing campaign** (Microsoft Security Blog, storm-2372, active Aug 2024, publicly reported Feb 13, 2025, escalation update Feb 14, 2025): Russia-aligned actor; targets government/NGO/IT/defense/telecom/health/energy sectors across Europe, North America, Africa, Middle East; method — impersonate a known contact on WhatsApp/Signal/Teams, send a fake meeting invite containing an attacker-generated device code, victim enters it on the legitimate Microsoft login page and hands over a valid access token without any password ever changing hands; post-compromise, attackers search victim mailboxes via Microsoft Graph API for keywords like "password," "admin," "credentials," "ministry." Feb 14 update: escalated to abusing Microsoft's own Authentication Broker client ID to register rogue devices into Entra ID and mint Primary Refresh Tokens. **No victim count disclosed** in the report. **Confidence: high** (primary source, though scale is genuinely unquantified by Microsoft itself, not just by me).
- A second confirmed device-code campaign found via HN: Microsoft Security Blog, **"An AI-enabled device code phishing campaign," published April 6 2026** — exists and is indexed, but I did not fetch its body; and a **"GitHub Device Code Phishing"** technique writeup from Praetorian (praetorian.com, June 2025) — also found via HN indexing but not fetched for detail. Both are **leads, not verified content** this session.
- **WebAuthn hybrid transport / caBLE**: confirmed to exist as "the WebAuthn Client accessing credentials stored on another authenticator... useful when credential sync is not viable" — i.e., the standardized answer to "authenticate on a device you don't own, using a phone you do own." Technical mechanics (BLE handshake, QR flow) and deployment/adoption numbers: **NOT REACHED.**
- **TOFU / SSH host keys** (Wikipedia "Trust on first use"): confirmed as the oldest deployed headless-provisioning pattern — client checks a local key cache, prompts on first contact, and on any later mismatch throws a blocking warning. Documented failure mode: "warning fatigue" causes users to blindly accept identifiers, and the model doesn't scale to many endpoints (an admin managing many hosts habituates past the warning). **Confidence: high** for the mechanism, **medium** for how well this generalizes as a critique (it's Wikipedia's own framing, not a cited study).
- **Matter commissioning** (csa-iot.org, Wikipedia "Matter (standard)", GitHub issue search on project-chip/connectedhomeip, HN comment search): confirmed BLE is used for commissioning and that a PKI with device attestation certificates underlies certification, but **the specific PAA/PAI/DAC hierarchy, the 11-digit setup code mechanics, and any measured commissioning failure rate or complaint volume were NOT REACHED** — GitHub issue search returned 1,209 total hits for "commissioning failed" against connectedhomeip but I only inspected one (an open PR adding NFC failsafe-timer tests, not a failure-rate data point), and HN comment search surfaced discussion threads (e.g., "how easy is it to commission a Matter device onto a specific WiFi network," Oct 2025; a guide for provisioning without a smartphone, July 2025) but no aggregate failure-rate numbers.
- **FIDO Device Onboard (FDO)**: confirmed release date (April 20, 2021, FIDO Alliance) but **adoption/deployment scale NOT REACHED** — HN search returned only date-stamped hits with no titles/URLs resolving meaningfully, and fidoalliance.org's own FDO page had no adoption metrics, just a certification-program description. The task brief's suspicion of "very low adoption" is **plausible but unconfirmed** by this session's evidence — flag as unverified, not as fact.
- **Apple Shared iPad** (education/business): **NOT REACHED** — every guessed support.apple.com URL 403'd or 404'd, and Wikipedia searches for it returned unrelated Apple hardware pages (Apple Wallet, Apple Watch, Face ID).
- **ChromeOS kiosk/managed guest sessions**: **NOT REACHED** — the one page that loaded was about factory-reset/device-wipe, not session/identity architecture.

### Summary of what's solid vs. open

**Solid (high confidence, dated, sourced):** Cisco per-capita device growth; Pew smartphone-only-by-income table (the single most important number for this model); ITU global ownership/connectivity figures; GSMA-2016 farmer phone-vs-mobile-money gap; London phone-theft annual totals 2019 vs 2024; RFC 8628's own phishing-risk acknowledgment; the Storm-2372 campaign mechanics and dates.

**Open (explicitly not reached, do not treat as answered):** Deloitte per-household device count; GSMA India/Bangladesh phone-sharing rates specifically; smartphone replacement-cycle length (conflicting weak signal only); Google/Microsoft account-lockout-after-device-loss rates; Matter commissioning failure rates and DAC/PAA PKI detail; FIDO Device Onboard real-world adoption scale; Apple Shared iPad mechanism and known problems; ChromeOS kiosk identity architecture; caBLE technical/adoption detail.
