I'll stop here — WebSearch quota was exhausted for this session almost immediately (200/200 before I could run a query), and further blind WebFetch guesses at unknown URLs are mostly returning 403/404. Here is the report based on what I was able to confirm.

## Report: Devices-per-person ground truth (partial — WebSearch was unavailable this session)

**Important caveat up front:** WebSearch returned "budget exhausted, 200/200" on the very first call and stayed exhausted for the whole session, so I could not discover fresh URLs — only WebFetch known/guessed URLs, most of which 403'd or 404'd (Deloitte, GSMA, BBC, Statista, ONS deep pages, Apple support, Forbes, gov.uk crime stats, Volexity, Counterpoint). What follows is confirmed from the fetches that succeeded. This is a thin subset of what was asked — recommend a follow-up pass with WebSearch available.

### 1. Devices per person — CONFIRMED (medium-high confidence)
- **Cisco Annual Internet Report**: global networked devices per capita rose from **2.4 (2018) to 3.6 (2023)**. Regional 2023 breakdown: North America 13.4/capita, Western Europe 9.4, CEE 4.0, Asia Pacific 3.1, Latin America 3.1, Middle East/Africa 1.5. Global device count: 18.4B (2018) → 29.3B (2023), 10% CAGR. Source: Cisco AIR white paper (cisco.com). This matches the number the user already had — confirmed, not new.
- Could NOT confirm Deloitte's ~21-22 devices/household figure — fetched the 2025 Connected Consumer Survey page, which reports **spending** ($896/year on connected devices, up from $764 in 2024) but not device counts in the section retrieved.

### 2. Smartphone-only / smartphone-dependent — CONFIRMED, high confidence, with a distribution the user should note
Pew Research (pewresearch.org/internet/fact-sheet/mobile/), by household income, "smartphone-only" (no home broadband):
| Income | 2013 | 2015 | 2018 | 2021 | 2025 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| <$30k | 12% | 20% | 31% | 27% | **34%** |
| $30-70k | 9% | 15% | 22% | 19% | 19% |
| $70-100k | 5% | 10% | 14% | 6% | 10% |
| $100k+ | 5% | 6% | 9% | 6% | 4% |

**This breaks the "several devices" assumption sharply along income lines**: over a third of low-income US households in 2025 are single-device (smartphone-only), and the trend for that bracket is worsening, not improving, while high-income households are trending toward MORE redundancy (4% and falling). This is a widening gap, not a converging one.

### 3. Global connectivity / device ownership — CONFIRMED, high confidence
ITU 2024 figures (itu.int):
- 5.5B online (68% of world), 2.6B offline (32%).
- Mobile ownership (age 10+): global ~4 in 5 own a phone; **high-income countries 95%+, low-income countries only 56%** — meaning ~44% of people in low-income countries have NO phone of their own at all (the true floor below "one device" is "zero devices, borrowed access").
- Gender gap: 70% of men online vs 65% of women (189M more men online).
- Urban 83% vs rural 48% online.
- 5G coverage: high-income 84%, low-income 4%.

I could not reach GSMA's Mobile Gender Gap Report directly (403 on all URL guesses) to get the specific phone-**sharing** rate numbers for India/Bangladesh/Africa the user wanted — that report (usually cited around 60%+ borrowing rates in some South Asian contexts, women disproportionately borrowing rather than owning) needs a follow-up fetch with search available.

### 4. Device loss/theft — PARTIALLY CONFIRMED
- UK ONS crime bulletin (year ending March 2025): "theft from the person" (a broader category than phones specifically) rose **15% to 151,220 offences**. Could not get a phone-specific breakout — ONS/police tables didn't resolve, and news sources (BBC, Forbes) were blocked from fetching.
- Could not reach Asurion, GSMA sharing data, or Google's 2019 account-hygiene research body text (only got the title/date: "New research: How effective is basic account hygiene," May 17, 2019 — the actual percentages didn't come through).

### 5. Shared/kiosk device patterns — PARTIALLY CONFIRMED
- **RFC 8628 (OAuth Device Authorization Grant)**, ietf.org/datatracker: confirmed core flow (device code + user code + verification URI + polling) and confirmed the spec's own Section 5.4 explicitly names phishing as a risk, telling implementers to make the user confirm device possession and to keep user-code lifetime short specifically "to limit the usability of a code obtained for phishing" — i.e., the phishing weakness is not incidental, it's spec-acknowledged.
- **Storm-2372 device-code phishing campaign** (Microsoft Security Blog, discovered Feb 2025, active since Aug 2024): Russia-aligned actor, targets across government/NGO/IT/defense/telecom/health/energy sectors in Europe, North America, Africa, Middle East. Method: build rapport via WhatsApp/Signal/Teams impersonating known contacts, send fake meeting invite, trick victim into entering an attacker-generated device code on the real Microsoft login page — victim hands over a valid access token without ever "losing" a password. Feb 14, 2025 update: attackers escalated to abusing Microsoft's own Authentication Broker client ID to register rogue devices into Entra ID and mint Primary Refresh Tokens. No victim count disclosed.
- **WebAuthn hybrid transport / caBLE**: confirmed it exists as "the WebAuthn Client accessing credentials stored on another authenticator... useful when credential sync is not viable" — i.e., the deployed answer to "authenticate on a device you don't own using a phone you do." Could not get caBLE's own technical/adoption specifics — Wikipedia's WebAuthn article didn't carry them.
- **TOFU (SSH host keys)**: confirmed as the oldest deployed pattern — client checks local key cache, prompts on first contact, warns loudly (and is often habituated-past) on mismatch thereafter. Known failure mode: "warning fatigue" causes users to blindly accept, and it doesn't scale to many endpoints.
- Apple Shared iPad, ChromeOS kiosk/managed guest sessions, Matter commissioning PKI (PAA/PAI/DAC) detail, and FIDO Device Onboard adoption data: all attempted, all came back as navigation/stub pages with no substantive numbers — genuine gaps, not just unlucky fetches.

### What I could NOT get at all this session
- GSMA Mobile Gender Gap sharing-rate numbers (India/Bangladesh/Africa)
- Deloitte's per-household device count
- Phone theft numbers specific to devices (vs. general "theft from person") for London/UK 2024-2025
- Smartphone replacement-cycle length figures (Counterpoint/CIRP/Kantar)
- Matter commissioning failure-rate/complaint data and its DAC/PAA PKI detail
- FIDO Device Onboard real-world adoption scale
- Apple Shared iPad's actual identity mechanism and known problems
- Google/Microsoft account-recovery lockout rate data

**Recommendation**: re-run this research with WebSearch available (this session's quota was already spent before I made a single call) — that tool is what would have surfaced the GSMA, Deloitte, Counterpoint, and phone-theft sources, none of which I could hit blind via WebFetch guesses.
````

